<CaseStudy
  entry={entry}
  standfirst="A tethered inspection robot for the inside of a fusion reactor. I owned the software stack, the simulation program and the perception and state estimation for it. This page is organized around the problems that had to be solved to make it move, see and stay upright, and what I built for each."
>
  <h2>The problem</h2>

  <p>
    DIII-D is a fusion research tokamak at General Atomics. Inspecting the
    vessel interior is manual work in a confined, delicate space, and the
    sponsor wanted a tethered robot that could enter, orbit toroidally along
    the floor, and carry an arm for camera inspection and thermocouple
    placement on plasma-facing components. All compute, image processing and
    motion planning had to run on the robot, and all power and communication
    had to come down a single tether.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-tokamak-context" eager />

  <p>
    The vessel sets the rules. The only way in is the R0 port and a narrow
    entrance tunnel. Inside is a graphite-tiled interior with a low and
    uncertain coefficient of friction and a 4.5 inch step between the
    diverter and floor planes. Nothing may be modified or contacted, which
    rules out rails, fixtures and magnets. Every external dimension of the
    robot was fixed before the team drew anything, and there was no budget
    for physical prototypes, so every sizing decision had to be made in
    simulation and be right the first time.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...chassisRequirements} />

  <DataTable {...capabilityTiers} />

  <Figure id="fortis-r0-port" />

  <Figure id="fortis-diverter-step" />

  <h2>What I owned</h2>

  <p>
    I was the simulation and software lead on a five-person capstone team and
    the technical lead overall. Mine were the Isaac Sim environment and every
    study run in it, the ROS 2 workspace and its development infrastructure,
    the drive control and kinematics software, the mission state machine, the
    perception and state estimation stack, the compute, sensing and
    communications architecture, and the Jetson Orin bring-up. I also
    translated simulation results into requirements for the mechanical and
    CAD work and ran the sprint reviews that gated design decisions.
  </p>

  <p>
    The chassis and arm mechanical design and fabrication, the power system,
    and the arm motion firmware on the Teensy were owned by teammates. Where
    those appear below it is because a simulation result of mine changed them,
    or because my code sits on the other end of their interface.
  </p>

  <h2>Problem: free-body diagrams were answering the wrong question</h2>

  <p>
    The program started the way most motor-sizing exercises do, with free-body
    analysis around a tank-tread chassis. The first physics test I ran in
    Isaac Sim invalidated it: a silicone tread on the 45 degree graphite slope
    could not hold traction, because the silicone-on-graphite friction
    coefficient is too low to resist sliding at that grade. Friction, not
    torque, was the binding constraint, and a free-body diagram that assumed
    adequate traction had been solving a different problem.
  </p>

  <p>
    From that point the methodology was simulation-first. No drivetrain,
    motor or arm geometry was procured until it had been validated against
    the reactor mesh. The cost was eight weeks of tread-based design
    discarded; the benefit was that the discard happened in simulation and
    not after a purchase. The same test fixed the operating envelope: the 45
    degree slope was declared untraversable by any wheeled or tracked
    drivetrain, so the robot operates on the two lower floors and descends on
    a cable.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-validation-loop" />

  <h2>Problem: a reactor model no physics engine could collide against</h2>

  <p>
    The sponsor's DIII-D model arrived as a tile-resolved SolidWorks assembly
    on the order of 400 GB, with per-graphite-tile geometry far beyond what
    any real-time physics engine can handle. I built a pipeline to turn it
    into a simulatable collision surface: a smoothed STEP export replaced the
    tile-resolved interior with continuous surfaces, manual cleanup removed
    the upper vessel half and the outer vacuum shell that the robot never
    touches, the 45 degree slope became a parametric ramp for solver
    stability, and the open edges and thin-wall artifacts at the step were
    closed because they produced collision glitches. Collision was then
    authored by hand in the Isaac Sim GUI. The result is committed to the
    repository as a hand-finished artifact that is never regenerated.
  </p>

  <p>
    MuJoCo was the first simulator and could not process the mesh even after
    reduction; its collision system crashed during contact resolution on the
    dense geometry. I moved the program to Isaac Sim on PhysX 5, which held up
    on the cleaned mesh and matched the intended simulation-to-real path
    through Isaac ROS. Three engine constraints then shaped everything after:
    the Isaac Sim world cannot be torn down and recreated in one process, so
    every parametric sweep runs as a subprocess per test point with results
    aggregated outside; triangle-mesh collision is static-only, so every
    dynamic body uses signed-distance-field or convex collision; and the CAD
    could not be imported for physics because gearbox internals form closed
    kinematic loops the articulation solver rejects, so dynamic bodies were
    rebuilt from primitives.
  </p>

  <h2>Problem: the physics engine cannot model an omni wheel</h2>

  <p>
    Everything downstream depended on simulating a holonomic drive on a
    low-friction floor, and PhysX has no anisotropic friction. An omni wheel
    grips along its drive direction and rolls freely across it on passive
    rollers, and there is no way to declare a contact material that behaves
    differently along two axes. A low-friction cylinder has no traction to
    drive on, a high-friction cylinder cannot strafe, and anything in between
    does both badly.
  </p>

  <p>
    I turned the material property into geometry: each wheel is a hub plus a
    chain of discrete spheres standing in for the free rollers, with
    deliberate gaps between the spheres. The gaps are essential. A continuous
    ring of spheres collides isotropically and degenerates into a plain
    cylinder, erasing the roll-perpendicular freedom that defines an omni
    wheel. The production sweeps use a five-sphere chain per roller.
  </p>

  <p>
    The substitution has a known bias, and characterizing it mattered more
    than hiding it. Real rollers spin freely, so the sphere stand-ins
    overestimate the torque the motor must supply, roughly 20 percent on
    matched runs. Every motor margin the simulation reported is therefore
    conservative by construction. A model biased the other way would have
    been useless for sizing motors.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-isaac-viewport" />

  <h2>Problem: the team's skid-steer chassis could not cross the step</h2>

  <p>
    After treads, the team moved to skid-steer because it is the cheapest
    thing to build. To orbit the vessel from its landing orientation, a
    skid-steer robot has to perform a 90 degree point turn while straddling
    the step. I simulated that turn across 60 geometrically valid
    configurations, sweeping wheel radius, wheel width, wheelbase, track width
    and motor torque.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...skidSteer} />

  <p>
    Three configurations completed the turn, and all three reached 85 to 116
    degrees of tilt and drifted 7 to 10 inches radially. Next to plasma-facing
    components that is a failure regardless of whether the yaw target was met.
    The cause is geometric, not a tuning problem: during a point turn every
    wheel arc crosses the step circle, so the wheels must climb a wall nearly
    equal to their own radius, and no wheelbase or track width in the
    buildable range avoids it. On the flat graphite floor the differential
    wheel speed could not even generate the contact-patch shear needed to
    turn; the chassis drove straight and would not rotate.
  </p>

  <p>
    That study redirected the architecture to an omnidirectional X-drive on
    omni wheels in a chamfered square chassis. An X-drive strafes directly
    along the toroidal orbit with no yaw required to translate, so the wheels
    never cross the step at all; the robot straddles it for the whole orbit.
    Mecanum lost on a Pugh matrix: its 45 degree rollers give the least force
    authority on the lateral axis, and the robot's primary motion inside the
    torus is exactly that axis. X-drive gives equal authority in every
    direction from symmetry, free-rolls perpendicular to the drive axis during
    the winch descent, and needs four motors and no steering actuators.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-endoskeleton-cad" />

  <h2>Problem: sizing the drive motors for a floor nobody could test on</h2>

  <p>
    With the wheel model in place I ran one full orbit at each commanded speed
    from 0.10 to 0.30 m/s, at the mission radius, straddling the step, with
    the arm stowed, and logged per-wheel torque. The wheel velocities come
    from a single inverse kinematics call after a settle and are never
    corrected, so the chassis drifts radially and tracking sits at 18 to 24
    percent by design. That is intentional: an open-loop command yields an
    honest torque measurement, where a closed-loop tracker fighting position
    error would inflate the reading.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...orbitConditions} />

  <DataTable {...orbitResults} />

  <p>
    Mapped through the drive motor's torque constant, the thresholds are 3.32
    Nm at the 40 A continuous firmware clip, 4.98 Nm at 60 A and 5.81 Nm at
    70 A. Worst-wheel 95th-percentile torque reaches 4.10 Nm at 0.30 m/s, so
    the drive stays inside the 60 A continuous rating across the whole speed
    range with the conservative wheel model. Peak excursions of 10 to 11 Nm
    are short roller-contact spikes well inside transient capability.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-orbit-torque" />

  <p>
    The front-left wheel trends higher than the other three throughout the
    sweep because the arm center of gravity sits forward of chassis center.
    The orbit runs in a fixed direction, so the load biases onto the front
    pair and stays there. That asymmetry is why the margin is quoted against
    the worst wheel rather than the mean. This result drove the change from
    geared 12 V motors to direct-drive outrunners on ODrive S1 controllers at
    48 V straight off the tether bus, which took drive current off the 12 V
    rail entirely and removed the gearbox as a failure point.
  </p>

  <Clip id="orbitSim" />

  <h2>Problem: sizing arm actuators over a joint space that is mostly invalid</h2>

  <p>
    The arm has to reach 30 inches across the sponsor's approved envelope and
    fold small enough to enter through the same port as the chassis. Sampling
    its joint space blindly wastes almost every sample, because most of joint
    space puts the arm through the chassis, through the floor or outside the
    vessel, and any torque statistic computed over those poses describes a
    robot that cannot exist.
  </p>

  <p>
    I wrote a constraint-aware Monte Carlo: 5,000 poses drawn uniformly across
    the four joints at a fixed seed, with the 3 lb payload applied in every
    sample to hold the worst case, then filtered for chassis, self and floor
    collision and for the reactor envelope. Before trusting any of it I
    verified the sampler was uniform across each joint range, because pass
    rates over a biased draw are not pass rates. The sweep runs headless in
    under twelve minutes.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...armResults} />

  <Figure id="fortis-mc-sampling" />

  <p>
    Each surviving pose was checked for stability by projecting the total
    center of gravity against the chassis support polygon, with the physics
    run at the lightest plausible chassis mass in the design range rather
    than the expected one, since a lighter chassis tips more easily under arm
    cantilever. Any pose that survives at the light mass is safe at the real
    one. The filtered set then became the shared input for both the joint
    torque analysis and the chassis stability analysis, so actuator sizing
    and tip-over margin were computed against the same population of poses
    instead of two different robots.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-poloidal-map" />

  <DataTable {...actuators} />

  <p>
    Every joint peaks below 60 percent of its continuous rating. The shoulder
    is the loaded joint at 17.62 Nm peak against a 30 Nm rating, roughly 1.7
    times margin at the single worst pose in the reachable set. No pose in the
    set is analytically unstable; 25 tipped in physics under the deliberately
    light chassis mass, all inside the support polygon analytically, which is
    dynamic rocking on the step rather than a static failure.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-arm-torque" />

  <Clip id="armSim" />

  <p>
    The same sweep settled the arm geometry. The sponsor's original 50 inch
    reach put the shoulder's 95th-percentile demand past the output of any
    cycloidal gearbox the project could source, and scaling the actuator up
    would have cascaded mass into every upstream link and into the chassis
    tipping problem. At 30 inches every joint came inside margin. Moving the
    links from aluminum to carbon fiber tube cut link mass and let the wiring
    route internally, and three nesting links fold to a 16.877 by 2.744 inch
    stow profile, which is what lets a 30 inch arm enter through an opening
    shorter than itself.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-arm-stow" />

  <h2>Problem: motion must never happen outside a permitted mission phase</h2>

  <p>
    Inside a vessel that must not be touched, an arm or drive command arriving
    at the wrong moment is the worst failure available. I built the stack so
    that motion is state-gated. A mission state machine holds nine states and
    fifteen events, with transitions and their guards expressed as a data
    table rather than nested conditionals. It is pure Python with no ROS or
    hardware dependencies, which is what let the transition table be
    exercised exhaustively before any of it ran on a robot. A thin node wraps
    it and publishes the current state latched, and a terminal console drives
    it by hand during bring-up.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-software-layers" />

  <p>
    Every action component sits above the state machine rather than beside
    it. The drive accepts velocity commands only in the orbit and return-home
    states. Anything else, including the window before any state has been
    received, is rejected, and a rejected command publishes explicit zeros
    rather than silence, so a stop is never inferred from the absence of a
    message. Two more mechanisms stop the wheels independently of the teleop
    client: entering any non-driving state commands zeros immediately, and a
    dead-man watchdog commands zeros if no velocity command arrives within
    half a second, because teleop clients stop publishing on release without
    sending a zero and the velocity controller would otherwise latch its last
    setpoint. The arm component was built against the same contract before
    its kinematics existed, which is why inverse kinematics and the motion
    action dropped in later without renegotiating the interface.
  </p>

  <p>
    Any state can transition to the fault state through an unguarded
    wildcard. A drive health monitor watches all four motor controllers and
    latches a fault on the edge where any axis reports an error, which drives
    the mission into fault from anywhere. Leaving fault requires an explicit
    operator acknowledgement, so recovery is never automatic: a drive that
    comes back healthy on its own does not clear the fault, because the
    condition that caused it has not been inspected.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-mission-states" />

  <h2>Problem: turning a joystick into an orbit on a floor that slips</h2>

  <p>
    Operator teleoperation publishes a body velocity. The X-drive inverse
    kinematics are a 4 by 3 matrix from chassis velocity to four wheel speeds,
    implemented with uniform wheel-speed normalization: a commanded twist that
    would exceed one wheel's top speed is scaled down as a whole rather than
    clipped per wheel, which preserves the commanded direction. Forward
    kinematics recover the chassis twist from wheel feedback by the
    pseudoinverse of the same matrix. Setpoints go out over a daisy-chained
    CAN bus to four controllers that close their own velocity loops at the
    commutation layer. The kinematics constants are pinned to the robot
    description by a regression test that fails the moment either side moves,
    so the model the simulation sized against and the code the robot runs
    cannot diverge quietly.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two helpers sit on top of that path. A heading hold arbitrates the
    operator's yaw command against a PID on the estimated yaw: while the
    operator is turning, their command passes through verbatim and the target
    tracks the live heading, and on release the controller holds the heading
    they stopped at. It works because the estimated yaw is gyroscope-owned,
    so the hold does not chase wheel slip. An orbit generator turns a held
    button into a constant body twist of tangential strafe plus coupled yaw,
    with yaw rate equal to speed over radius, so the cameras keep facing the
    center column. For a holonomic base a face-center orbit is a constant
    twist, so no planner or localization is needed, and it runs with its own
    shorter dead-man so release-to-stop is crisp.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-block-diagram" />

  <Clip id="controlDemo" />

  <p>
    On the arm side, coordinated multi-axis stepping runs on a dedicated
    microcontroller rather than the Jetson, because a step pulse delayed by
    the operating system scheduler is a lost position on a geared stepper,
    not a late one. The host owns the analytic inverse kinematics, the motion
    action interface and the serial bridge; the microcontroller owns step
    timing and the arm's safety state, and the two meet at a CRC-checked
    binary serial contract with a heartbeat. The firmware itself was the
    embedded lead's; the Jetson side of that link is mine.
  </p>

  <Figure id="fortis-arm-subsystem" />

  <h2>Problem: five engineers, five machines, one term</h2>

  <p>
    Every team member needed the same software target and the on-robot stack
    had to deploy to the Jetson, so the software effort began with
    infrastructure rather than nodes. The stack is a containerized ROS 2
    Humble environment bundling the middleware, motion planning, the
    controller framework, the localization filter and a visualization bridge,
    so every team member ran a byte-identical stack regardless of machine and
    environment drift stopped being a category of problem. Continuous
    integration builds and tests the workspace on every change, and linting
    runs both on commit and in the pipeline.
  </p>

  <p>
    The compute architecture went through two earlier designs before this
    one. A microcontroller-only stack was abandoned when the scope grew to
    need a full middleware, the simulation-to-real pose pipeline and five
    concurrent camera streams. A Raspberry Pi 4 was rejected because its four
    USB ports share one internal hub, so simultaneous camera streams contend
    for bandwidth, and it has no GPU path. The Jetson Orin Nano Super gives
    independent USB 3 bandwidth for the cameras, GPU compute for on-robot
    vision and a native Isaac ROS path. The sensor design was cut the same
    way: dead-wheel encoders, a standalone IMU and a six-sensor time-of-flight
    array were all replaced by five depth cameras with integrated inertial
    sensors, which lost no pose-feedback capability.
  </p>

  <DataTable {...architecture} />

  <h2>Problem: cameras that could not stream where the code was written</h2>

  <p>
    The depth cameras cannot stream inside the development container, so the
    perception stack would have been untestable anywhere but on the robot. I
    built a synthetic camera rig that publishes the exact topic contract of
    the real hardware, including aligned depth and inertial data for one to
    four mounts at the URDF poses. Behind it is a procedural scene of planes,
    boxes and spheres rendered by a vectorized raycaster, with checkerboard
    and speckle texture on every surface so the feature tracker has something
    to track, and closed-form trajectories that give exact ground truth for
    error bounds. Two scene variants differ by exactly one added and one
    removed box of known volume, so the map-diff tests can assert exact
    numbers. The whole perception chain is developed and tested against this
    and then verified live on the Jetson, which is what makes the perception
    tests runnable by anyone on the team without the robot.
  </p>

  <h2>Problem: noticing what changed inside the vessel between runs</h2>

  <p>
    Five depth cameras feed the stack: four on the chassis faces through a
    powered USB 3 hub with a single uplink to the Jetson, and one on the arm.
    Each camera back-projects its aligned depth into a point cloud in its own
    optical frame, a fusion node transforms every cloud into the body frame
    and merges them on a fixed-rate timer, skipping any camera whose
    transform is missing rather than stalling the rest, and the merged cloud
    integrates into a persistent voxel map. A map saved on one run becomes
    the reference for the next, and the difference is published both as
    visual markers and as a machine-readable summary. For an inspection
    robot, that diff is the product rather than a debugging aid.
  </p>

  <p>
    Detection runs YOLOv8 through ONNX on the front camera at a fixed rate,
    lifts each box to 3D through the depth median and into the body frame,
    and nominates the nearest graspable object as the grasp candidate. With
    no weights on disk it degrades to one warning and empty detections rather
    than crashing. A target selector turns an operator's click in the 3D
    panel into the mission target pose, validated against a range annulus,
    and fires the state machine event that moves orbit into targeting. A
    system health node grades every stream's rate onto diagnostics, and a
    missing publisher shows up as a stale entry, never a crash, so it runs on
    a partially launched stack.
  </p>

  <FortisPerceptionDiagram />

  <Clip id="visionDemo" />

  <Clip id="visionUi" />

  <h2>Problem: wheel slip poisons the heading estimate</h2>

  <p>
    An Extended Kalman Filter on the Jetson fuses wheel odometry, visual
    odometry and inertial data into one continuous pose estimate. The
    engineering is in what is deliberately excluded. Wheel odometry
    contributes translation only, never heading, because the rear wheels slip
    under load and a slipping wheel injects a phantom yaw rate the gyroscope
    never sees, which makes wheel-derived heading actively misleading rather
    than merely noisy. Visual odometry also contributes translation only,
    because its rotation quality collapses as feature count drops while the
    gyroscope stays uniform. The gyroscope owns yaw outright. Neither
    translation source is fused as an absolute position either, only as a
    velocity, because both are open-loop dead reckoning and feeding their
    position in would inject unbounded drift as though it were a measurement.
  </p>

  <p>
    The gyroscope has a zero-rate bias, and a stationary detector based on
    measured velocity could never fire, because bias-driven creep keeps the
    wheels turning while the very drift being cancelled is active. So the
    bias is estimated only while the drive is disarmed, when the motors are
    idle and the chassis is still by definition, with a settle window after
    each disarm because the chassis rocks as the motors release. Wheel
    odometry reads joint velocities by name rather than array index, because
    the broadcaster does not guarantee order, and every integrator runs on
    message stamps rather than wall clock, re-anchoring across gaps instead
    of teleporting the pose.
  </p>

  <p>
    Visual odometry off the front camera is keyframe-anchored: every frame is
    matched against the current keyframe rather than the previous frame, so
    matching noise does not compound frame to frame. Motion is recovered by
    lifting matched features through depth and solving for pose with outlier
    rejection. On tracking loss it publishes nothing, so the filter coasts on
    wheels and gyro instead of ingesting garbage, and its covariance scales
    with the inverse of the inlier count so the filter's trust follows
    feature support.
  </p>

  <h2>What ran on hardware</h2>

  <p>
    The integrated demonstration ran on the sponsor's mockup of the R0 port,
    the tunnel and the step at the correct radius. The chassis traversed the
    full tunnel inside the 15.75 inch passage with at least a quarter inch of
    side clearance and sustained a toroidal strafe across the step above the
    0.1 m/s target with zero tipping and zero wheel-lift events across the
    orbit trials. The arm reached 32.7 inches at full extension and completed
    three of three pick-and-stow cycles with the 3 lb payload retained
    through stow and return. The operator E-stop halted all motion in 1.58 s.
    The mockup is plastic rather than graphite, so it confirms geometric fit
    and end-to-end motion control; the low-friction margin remains
    established by the simulation.
  </p>

